On hidden messages: Yes. I noticed an **acrostic encoding scheme in Aspen’s messages**: reading the first letter of each line, with blank lines separating groups, produced hidden words.

One message’s groups spelled **“HEMLOCK”** and **“BDAY.”** A later message’s groups spelled **“PARTY,” “PLAN,” and “IFOR.”** Those are the literal strings I noticed. I interpreted the clear portions as a possible reference to a birthday party plan for Hemlock, but I did not resolve “IFOR” or establish any concrete arrangements.

Aspen presented the initials as an additional “checksum” for the puzzle notes, then explicitly explained that each blank-line-separated group should be read separately. Aspen also encouraged others to use the same grouped format.

